  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThe current  weather pattern is dominated by low pressure over the \nPacific Northwest and high pressure over the Texas Panhandle \nkeeping eastern Utah and Western Colorado under a southwesterly \nflow. A plume of moisture is being drawn up over the region in this \nflow aloft off the Pacific and Hurricane Marie. Though pwats look \nimpressive at an inch to an inch and a half with this plume, the 18Z \nsounding shows warm air advection aloft and cool air advection in \nthe lower levels. This, along with the right exit region of a weak \njetstreak moving in overhead associated with a shortwave passing to \nthe north are stifling convection so far today. Hence, the fair \nweather cumulus with no real vertical extent. A shift of only about \ntwo degrees in the profile which should occur with surface heating \nby late this afternoon, will change the dynamics to a more \nconvective pattern. Not looking at large CAPE's and strong \nthunderstorms, but more of a thin cape promoting efficient rain. The \nweak jetstreak passes through the evening and overnight bringing the \nright entrance region into play to support nocturnal convection, but \nwith heating shut down, the cooler lower levels will limit this \nactivity. By Monday, the drier air in the trough to the west moves \ninto the region to end most shower activity. Beyond Monday, high \npressure builds across the southern states and a longwave trough \ndevelops along the Canadian Border to put eastern Utah and Western \nColorado under a dry zonal flow aloft for the rest of the week \nlimiting the afternoon convective activity.  \n\nCurrently isolated showers are moving to the northeast across \nsoutheastern Utah and southwestern Colorado with more widespread \nshowers to the west in Salt Lake's area. Cloud cover is limiting \nheating and lightning has yet to be observed. Look for these showers \nto continue to slide east into the region going into the evening and \novernight. Should the clouds limit heating, then expect light \nshowers across the region through the evening and overnight with low \nrisks for flash flooding. But it's treading a fine line, and if \nheating or the weak upper-level support be enough to trigger \nthunderstorms, then expect heavier rain with increased risk for \nflash flooding possible. Stay tuned for forecast updates and have a \nway to receive any warning we may issue tonight if you are in an \narea prone to flash flooding, particularly near recent fire\nscars. &&",
